--->  Tech Stack

Frontend: HTML, CSS, JavaScript, Tailwind CSS, Font Awesome
Backend: Python, FastAPI
AI: Groq API + openai/gpt-oss-120b
Agents: Cold Mail Agent, Digging/Research Agent
Web Extraction: LangChain WebBaseLoader
Database: MySQL + SQLAlchemy
Deployment Configuration: Vercel frontend configuration + backend Procfile

--->  Installation & Setup
git clone <repository-url>
cd PitStop

python -m venv venv

# Windows
venv\Scripts\activate

pip install -r requirements.txt

Install Node dependencies if required:

npm install

Create/configure .env with the actual credentials required by the project's configuration, including database and AI API credentials.

--->  Database Setup
Install and start MySQL.
Create the required database.
Configure the database connection in .env.
Start the application so SQLAlchemy can access the configured database.
Ensure the required user and portfolio tables are available.

--->  Run

From the project root:

python main.py

Then open the local FastAPI application in your browser using the host/port shown by the server.

--->  Main Application Routes
/              → Landing page
/login         → Login
/register      → Registration
/dashboard     → Dashboard
/portfolio_store → Portfolio management
/generator     → AI cold-email generator
/company       → Company research

---> Important API Endpoints
POST   /login
POST   /register
GET    /api/portfolio
POST   /api/portfolio
GET    /api/portfolio/{id}
PUT    /api/portfolio/{id}
DELETE /api/portfolio/{id}
POST   /email
POST   /info

---> Current Scope

Implemented: Authentication, portfolio CRUD, job scraping, job extraction, AI project matching, cold-email generation, company research, MySQL persistence, responsive frontend.

Not implemented in the supplied source: Actual email sending, delivery tracking, hiring-email discovery, application-history/analytics system, resume-processing workflow, and automated outreach execution.

---> Setup Notes
Keep API keys and database credentials out of Git.
Verify the database configuration before running.
The current implementation should be treated as the source of truth; documentation files may describe planned functionality that is not yet implemented.
Do not assume planned features are available unless they exist in the supplied code.